00:02:17,583 --> 00:02:18,384
The numbers.

70
00:02:18,564 --> 00:02:21,500
Seven lessons about how index funds actually

71
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:23,835
work, and why the math favors them.

72
00:02:24,185 --> 00:02:25,393
Lesson number one.

73
00:02:25,573 --> 00:02:28,044
An index fund buys the whole market,

74
00:02:28,044 --> 00:02:29,485
or a big slice of it.

75
00:02:29,835 --> 00:02:31,710
An index is just a list of

76
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:34,305
companies, chosen by a set of rules.

77
00:02:34,485 --> 00:02:36,117
The S and P five hundred,

78
00:02:36,117 --> 00:02:38,792
for example, tracks about five hundred of

79
00:02:38,792 --> 00:02:41,664
the largest publicly traded companies in the

80
00:02:41,664 --> 00:02:42,577
United States.

81
00:02:42,757 --> 00:02:44,900
An index fund simply buys all the

82
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:46,393
companies on that list,

83
00:02:46,393 --> 00:02:48,471
in roughly the same proportions,

84
00:02:48,471 --> 00:02:50,873
so its performance follows the index.

85
00:02:51,222 --> 00:02:53,083
So when you buy one share of

86
00:02:53,083 --> 00:02:55,011
an S and P five hundred index

87
00:02:55,011 --> 00:02:56,938
fund, you own a tiny piece of

88
00:02:56,938 --> 00:02:58,931
hundreds of companies at once.

89
00:02:59,111 --> 00:03:01,437
Technology companies, banks,

90
00:03:01,437 --> 00:03:03,348
retailers, health care,

91
00:03:03,348 --> 00:03:03,930
energy.

92
00:03:04,110 --> 00:03:06,342
One purchase gives you a slice of

93
00:03:06,342 --> 00:03:08,845
a large part of the American economy,

94
00:03:08,845 --> 00:03:11,552
instead of betting on a single business.

95
00:03:11,901 --> 00:03:14,386
There are index funds for almost everything.

96
00:03:14,566 --> 00:03:15,576
Total U.

97
00:03:15,756 --> 00:03:16,104
S.

98
00:03:16,284 --> 00:03:19,567
stock market funds hold thousands of companies,

99
00:03:19,567 --> 00:03:20,685
large and small.

100
00:03:20,865 --> 00:03:24,286
International index funds hold companies outside the

101
00:03:24,286 --> 00:03:25,207
United States.

102
00:03:25,387 --> 00:03:28,428
Bond index funds hold thousands of bonds.

103
00:03:28,608 --> 00:03:30,652
The idea is always the same.

104
00:03:30,832 --> 00:03:32,248
Don't try to pick winners.

105
00:03:32,428 --> 00:03:33,903
Own the whole haystack.

106
00:03:34,252 --> 00:03:37,072
Index funds come as mutual funds or

107
00:03:37,072 --> 00:03:39,085
as exchange traded funds,

108
00:03:39,085 --> 00:03:40,696
usually called ETFs.

109
00:03:40,876 --> 00:03:43,448
ETFs trade during the day like stocks,

110
00:03:43,448 --> 00:03:45,817
while mutual funds trade once a day

111
00:03:45,817 --> 00:03:47,238
at the closing price.

112
00:03:47,418 --> 00:03:48,996
For a long term investor,

113
00:03:48,996 --> 00:03:50,321
either can work well.

114
00:03:50,501 --> 00:03:52,310
The fund's cost and what it holds

115
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:54,065
matter far more than the format.

116
00:03:54,414 --> 00:03:55,564
Lesson number two.

117
00:03:55,744 --> 00:03:58,019
The idea Wall Street laughed at.

118
00:03:58,369 --> 00:04:02,607
In 1975, John Bogle founded Vanguard.

119
00:04:02,787 --> 00:04:03,717
The next year,

120
00:04:03,717 --> 00:04:06,174
his company launched one of the first

121
00:04:06,174 --> 00:04:09,562
index mutual funds available to ordinary investors,

122
00:04:09,562 --> 00:04:11,820
tracking the S and P five hundred.

123
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:13,033
Critics mocked it.

124
00:04:13,213 --> 00:04:15,291
Some called it Bogle's folly.

125
00:04:15,471 --> 00:04:17,883
Why would anyone settle for average returns,

126
00:04:17,883 --> 00:04:19,692
they asked, when you could hire a

127
00:04:19,692 --> 00:04:21,172
manager to beat the market?

128
00:04:21,521 --> 00:04:24,273
Bogle's answer was simple arithmetic.

129
00:04:24,453 --> 00:04:27,252
Before costs, all investors together earn the

130
00:04:27,252 --> 00:04:30,113
market's return, because together they are the

131
00:04:30,113 --> 00:04:30,548
market.

132
00:04:30,728 --> 00:04:33,630
After costs, the average investor must earn

133
00:04:33,630 --> 00:04:35,047
less than the market.

134
00:04:35,227 --> 00:04:37,522
So a fund that charges very little

135
00:04:37,522 --> 00:04:40,290
and simply matches the market should beat

136
00:04:40,290 --> 00:04:42,180
most investors who pay more,

137
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:42,855
over time.

138
00:04:43,204 --> 00:04:44,414
It took decades,

139
00:04:44,414 --> 00:04:46,002
but Bogle's idea won.

140
00:04:46,182 --> 00:04:48,170
According to Morningstar,

141
00:04:48,170 --> 00:04:49,283
by early 2024,

142
00:04:49,283 --> 00:04:52,224
assets in passive funds in the United

143
00:04:52,224 --> 00:04:54,370
States, mostly index funds,

144
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:58,106
had overtaken assets in actively managed funds.

145
00:04:58,286 --> 00:05:00,062
The folly became the mainstream.

146
00:05:00,412 --> 00:05:01,156
Try this.

147
00:05:01,336 --> 00:05:03,536
Next time someone tells you they have

148
00:05:03,536 --> 00:05:05,321
a strategy to beat the market,

149
00:05:05,321 --> 00:05:06,630
ask a simple question.

150
00:05:06,810 --> 00:05:08,927
Have you beaten a low cost index

151
00:05:08,927 --> 00:05:11,111
fund over the last fifteen years,

152
00:05:11,111 --> 00:05:12,104
after all fees?

153
00:05:12,284 --> 00:05:14,766
Most people, and most professionals,

154
00:05:14,766 --> 00:05:15,732
can't say yes.

155
00:05:16,081 --> 00:05:17,300
Lesson number three.

156
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:20,174
Costs matter more than almost anything.

157
00:05:20,523 --> 00:05:23,211
Every fund charges a yearly fee called

158
00:05:23,211 --> 00:05:24,413
an expense ratio.

159
00:05:24,593 --> 00:05:27,644
According to the Investment Company Institute,

160
00:05:27,644 --> 00:05:30,297
in recent years the average index equity

161
00:05:30,297 --> 00:05:33,282
mutual fund charged around five hundredths of

162
00:05:33,282 --> 00:05:36,267
a percent, while the average actively managed

163
00:05:36,267 --> 00:05:39,450
equity fund charged around sixty five hundredths

164
00:05:39,450 --> 00:05:40,313
of a percent.

165
00:05:40,493 --> 00:05:41,863
That difference sounds tiny.

166
00:05:42,043 --> 00:05:42,716
It isn't.

167
00:05:43,066 --> 00:05:45,902
Imagine investing five hundred dollars a month

168
00:05:45,902 --> 00:05:48,122
for forty years with a seven percent

169
00:05:48,122 --> 00:05:50,218
average yearly return before fees.

170
00:05:50,398 --> 00:05:52,652
With a five hundredths of a percent

171
00:05:52,652 --> 00:05:55,035
fee, you'd end with roughly one point

172
00:05:55,035 --> 00:05:56,644
two nine million dollars.

173
00:05:56,824 --> 00:05:59,143
With a sixty five hundredths of a

174
00:05:59,143 --> 00:06:02,165
percent fee, roughly one point one million.

175
00:06:02,345 --> 00:06:05,182
That's around two hundred thousand dollars lost

176
00:06:05,182 --> 00:06:05,665
to fees.

177
00:06:06,015 --> 00:06:07,107
Why so much?

178
00:06:07,287 --> 00:06:09,272
Because fees compound too.

179
00:06:09,452 --> 00:06:11,370
Every dollar taken in fees is a

180
00:06:11,370 --> 00:06:13,225
dollar that never grows again,

181
00:06:13,225 --> 00:06:15,328
along with all the growth it would

182
00:06:15,328 --> 00:06:16,813
have earned for decades.

183
00:06:16,993 --> 00:06:18,333
And unlike returns,

184
00:06:18,333 --> 00:06:19,744
fees are guaranteed.

185
00:06:19,924 --> 00:06:21,635
You pay them in good years and

186
00:06:21,635 --> 00:06:21,863
bad.

187
00:06:22,213 --> 00:06:23,073
Try this.

188
00:06:23,253 --> 00:06:25,697
Look up the expense ratio of every

189
00:06:25,697 --> 00:06:26,631
fund you own.

190
00:06:26,811 --> 00:06:28,727
It's listed on the fund's page.

191
00:06:28,907 --> 00:06:31,041
If any are above half a percent,

192
00:06:31,041 --> 00:06:33,376
check whether a low cost index fund

193
00:06:33,376 --> 00:06:35,710
covers the same part of the market.

194
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:37,314
Lesson number four.

195
00:06:37,494 --> 00:06:40,072
Why most professionals lose to the index.

196
00:06:40,421 --> 00:06:42,627
It's not that fund managers are bad

197
00:06:42,627 --> 00:06:43,510
at their jobs.

198
00:06:43,690 --> 00:06:46,117
It's that beating the market is incredibly

199
00:06:46,117 --> 00:06:46,406
hard.

200
00:06:46,586 --> 00:06:48,933
Every time a professional buys a stock,

201
00:06:48,933 --> 00:06:51,521
another professional is usually selling it.

202
00:06:51,701 --> 00:06:53,036
They can't both be right.

203
00:06:53,216 --> 00:06:56,206
Together, they're competing against each other,

204
00:06:56,206 --> 00:06:58,115
and the competition is fierce.

205
00:06:58,465 --> 00:06:59,870
Then come the costs.

206
00:07:00,050 --> 00:07:02,111
Active funds pay for research,

207
00:07:02,111 --> 00:07:04,172
analysts and frequent trading.

208
00:07:04,352 --> 00:07:06,520
Those costs come out of returns every

209
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:06,813
year.

210
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:09,501
So even a manager who picks slightly

211
00:07:09,501 --> 00:07:12,078
better stocks than average can end up

212
00:07:12,078 --> 00:07:15,074
behind the index once costs are subtracted.

213
00:07:15,423 --> 00:07:17,121
And even the funds that do win

214
00:07:17,121 --> 00:07:18,535
often don't keep winning.

215
00:07:18,715 --> 00:07:21,174
S and P Dow Jones Indices also

216
00:07:21,174 --> 00:07:23,879
publishes persistence scorecards,

217
00:07:23,879 --> 00:07:27,568
which track whether top performing funds stay

218
00:07:27,568 --> 00:07:28,142
on top.

219
00:07:28,322 --> 00:07:30,644
Historically, relatively few do.

220
00:07:30,824 --> 00:07:33,018
Picking last year's winning fund is no

221
00:07:33,018 --> 00:07:34,922
guarantee of picking next year's.

222
00:07:35,272 --> 00:07:36,062
Try this.

223
00:07:36,242 --> 00:07:38,804
If you're paying for an actively managed

224
00:07:38,804 --> 00:07:41,559
fund, compare its fifteen year return after

225
00:07:41,559 --> 00:07:43,545
fees with a similar index fund.

226
00:07:43,725 --> 00:07:46,023
Numbers usually settle the debate quickly.

227
00:07:46,373 --> 00:07:47,813
Lesson number five.

228
00:07:47,993 --> 00:07:50,478
Diversification you get almost for free.

229
00:07:50,827 --> 00:07:52,864
When you own one company's stock,

230
00:07:52,864 --> 00:07:55,332
your money depends on that one business.

231
00:07:55,512 --> 00:07:56,330
If it fails,

232
00:07:56,330 --> 00:07:58,716
you can lose everything you put in.

233
00:07:58,896 --> 00:08:01,668
History is full of companies that once

234
00:08:01,668 --> 00:08:05,607
seemed unstoppable and later collapsed or disappeared.

235
00:08:05,957 --> 00:08:08,662
An index fund spreads your money across

236
00:08:08,662 --> 00:08:11,089
hundreds or thousands of companies.

237
00:08:11,269 --> 00:08:12,283
If one fails,

238
00:08:12,283 --> 00:08:14,624
it's a tiny piece of the fund.

239
00:08:14,804 --> 00:08:17,802
Meanwhile, the companies that grow into giants

240
00:08:17,802 --> 00:08:20,865
automatically become bigger parts of the index.

241
00:08:21,045 --> 00:08:22,772
You don't need to know in advance

242
00:08:22,772 --> 00:08:23,924
which ones they'll be.

243
00:08:24,273 --> 00:08:27,206
Research by Hendrik Bessembinder found that a

244
00:08:27,206 --> 00:08:29,618
small share of stocks created most of

245
00:08:29,618 --> 00:08:30,009
the U.

246
00:08:30,189 --> 00:08:30,595
S.

247
00:08:30,775 --> 00:08:33,078
stock market's long term wealth,

248
00:08:33,078 --> 00:08:36,172
while many individual stocks did worse than

249
00:08:36,172 --> 00:08:39,123
safe Treasury bills over their lifetimes.

250
00:08:39,303 --> 00:08:41,307
Owning the whole market is the easiest

251
00:08:41,307 --> 00:08:42,783
way to make sure you own the

252
00:08:42,783 --> 00:08:43,680
rare big winners.

253
00:08:44,029 --> 00:08:44,854
Try this.

254
00:08:45,034 --> 00:08:47,052
If most of your investments are in

255
00:08:47,052 --> 00:08:48,121
one or two stocks,

256
00:08:48,121 --> 00:08:49,724
especially your employer's,

257
00:08:49,724 --> 00:08:51,861
think about whether you'd be okay if

258
00:08:51,861 --> 00:08:53,939
that company had a terrible decade.

259
00:08:54,119 --> 00:08:55,419
If the answer is no,

260
00:08:55,419 --> 00:08:57,694
an index fund can spread that risk.

261
00:08:58,044 --> 00:08:59,531
Lesson number six.

262
00:08:59,711 --> 00:09:02,137
Index funds are often tax efficient.

263
00:09:02,486 --> 00:09:04,417
When a fund sells investments at a

264
00:09:04,417 --> 00:09:06,745
profit, it may have to distribute capital

265
00:09:06,745 --> 00:09:08,222
gains to its shareholders.

266
00:09:08,402 --> 00:09:10,894
In a regular taxable brokerage account,

267
00:09:10,894 --> 00:09:13,514
you can owe taxes on those distributions,

268
00:09:13,514 --> 00:09:15,559
even if you didn't sell a single

269
00:09:15,559 --> 00:09:16,517
share yourself.

270
00:09:16,867 --> 00:09:19,080
Active funds often trade a lot,

271
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:21,791
which can create more of these taxable

272
00:09:21,791 --> 00:09:22,220
gains.

273
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:24,680
Index funds usually trade much less,

274
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:27,341
because they only change holdings when the

275
00:09:27,341 --> 00:09:28,228
index changes.

276
00:09:28,408 --> 00:09:32,007
Less trading usually means fewer taxable distributions.

277
00:09:32,356 --> 00:09:35,003
Many index ETFs are especially tax efficient

278
00:09:35,003 --> 00:09:37,229
because of how ETF shares are created

279
00:09:37,229 --> 00:09:38,010
and redeemed.

280
00:09:38,190 --> 00:09:40,812
That's one reason they're popular for taxable

281
00:09:40,812 --> 00:09:41,337
accounts.

282
00:09:41,517 --> 00:09:44,242
In retirement accounts like a four oh

283
00:09:44,242 --> 00:09:45,200
one k or IRA,

284
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:46,526
this matters less,

285
00:09:46,526 --> 00:09:49,399
because growth there is tax deferred or

286
00:09:49,399 --> 00:09:50,062
tax free.

287
00:09:50,411 --> 00:09:51,178
Try this.

288
00:09:51,358 --> 00:09:53,638
If you invest in a regular taxable

289
00:09:53,638 --> 00:09:56,657
account, check your funds' history of capital

290
00:09:56,657 --> 00:09:57,999
gains distributions.

291
00:09:58,179 --> 00:10:00,902
Low distributions mean more of your money

292
00:10:00,902 --> 00:10:02,962
stays invested and compounding.

293
00:10:03,311 --> 00:10:04,635
Lesson number seven.

294
00:10:04,815 --> 00:10:06,742
Boring doesn't mean risk free.

295
00:10:07,092 --> 00:10:08,462
Here's an honest warning.

296
00:10:08,642 --> 00:10:11,136
An index fund follows the market,

297
00:10:11,136 --> 00:10:13,553
and that includes the bad times.

298
00:10:13,733 --> 00:10:15,642
In 2008, the S and P five

299
00:10:15,642 --> 00:10:18,238
hundred fell by more than a third.

300
00:10:18,418 --> 00:10:19,587
In early 2020,

301
00:10:19,587 --> 00:10:22,341
it dropped sharply in a matter of

302
00:10:22,341 --> 00:10:22,841
weeks.

303
00:10:23,021 --> 00:10:25,808
An index fund doesn't avoid these drops.

304
00:10:25,988 --> 00:10:27,068
It goes through them.

305
00:10:27,417 --> 00:10:29,356
Historically, the broad U.

306
00:10:29,536 --> 00:10:29,908
S.

307
00:10:30,088 --> 00:10:32,779
market has recovered from its past crashes

308
00:10:32,779 --> 00:10:34,381
and gone on to new highs,

309
00:10:34,381 --> 00:10:36,880
though recoveries sometimes took years.

310
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:39,341
That's why index funds work best for

311
00:10:39,341 --> 00:10:41,306
money you won't need for a long

312
00:10:41,306 --> 00:10:43,271
time, often five years or more.

313
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:46,043
Bond index funds and cash can balance

314
00:10:46,043 --> 00:10:48,073
the ups and downs for money you

315
00:10:48,073 --> 00:10:50,430
need sooner, or for people who can't

316
00:10:50,430 --> 00:10:51,608
stomach big drops.

317
00:10:51,788 --> 00:10:53,388
Many investors hold a mix,

318
00:10:53,388 --> 00:10:55,295
more stocks when they're young,

319
00:10:55,295 --> 00:10:57,264
more bonds as they get closer to

320
00:10:57,264 --> 00:10:58,371
needing the money.

321
00:10:58,720 --> 00:10:59,684
Try this.

322
00:10:59,864 --> 00:11:01,547
Ask yourself honestly.

323
00:11:01,727 --> 00:11:04,421
If my investments fell thirty percent next

324
00:11:04,421 --> 00:11:05,640
year, would I sell?

325
00:11:05,820 --> 00:11:07,808
If yes, you may want more bonds

326
00:11:07,808 --> 00:11:08,577
in your mix,

327
00:11:08,577 --> 00:11:10,885
so you can stay invested through the

328
00:11:10,885 --> 00:11:11,590
hard times.

329
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:12,950
That's angle one.

330
00:11:13,130 --> 00:11:14,821
What an index fund is,

331
00:11:14,821 --> 00:11:16,435
Bogle's folly, costs,

332
00:11:16,435 --> 00:11:17,818
why the pros lose,

333
00:11:17,818 --> 00:11:21,430
diversification, taxes, and boring but not risk

334
00:11:21,430 --> 00:11:21,815
free.

335
00:11:21,995 --> 00:11:23,272
Now angle two.

336
00:11:23,452 --> 00:11:24,474
The psychology.

337
00:11:24,823 --> 00:11:26,979
If index funds are so effective,

338
00:11:26,979 --> 00:11:29,539
why doesn't everyone just buy them and

339
00:11:29,539 --> 00:11:29,943
relax?

340
00:11:30,123 --> 00:11:32,748
Because human brains crave excitement,

341
00:11:32,748 --> 00:11:34,129
control and stories.

342
00:11:34,309 --> 00:11:36,352
Index investing offers none of that.

343
00:11:36,532 --> 00:11:37,408
In this angle,

344
00:11:37,408 --> 00:11:40,412
we'll look at seven psychological reasons boring

345
00:11:40,412 --> 00:11:41,538
investing is hard,

346
00:11:41,538 --> 00:11:43,666
and why getting past them is where

347
00:11:43,666 --> 00:11:45,356
the real returns come from.

348
00:11:45,705 --> 00:11:46,890
Lesson number eight.

349
00:11:47,070 --> 00:11:48,823
Our brains want excitement.

350
00:11:49,172 --> 00:11:51,134
Picking stocks feels exciting.

351
00:11:51,314 --> 00:11:52,745
Researching a company,

352
00:11:52,745 --> 00:11:54,565
buying it, watching it jump.

353
00:11:54,745 --> 00:11:55,686
It's like a game.

354
00:11:55,866 --> 00:11:58,291
Buying an index fund and leaving it

355
00:11:58,291 --> 00:12:00,440
alone feels like doing nothing.

356
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:02,847
And many people assume that doing nothing

357
00:12:02,847 --> 00:12:03,824
must be a mistake.

358
00:12:04,174 --> 00:12:07,889
Economist Paul Samuelson reportedly said investing should

359
00:12:07,889 --> 00:12:10,104
be more like watching paint dry or

360
00:12:10,104 --> 00:12:11,407
watching grass grow.

361
00:12:11,587 --> 00:12:13,000
If you want excitement,

362
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,702
he suggested, take eight hundred dollars and

363
00:12:15,702 --> 00:12:16,684
go to Las Vegas.

364
00:12:16,864 --> 00:12:19,301
His point was that excitement is often

365
00:12:19,301 --> 00:12:20,263
a sign of risk,

366
00:12:20,263 --> 00:12:20,904
not skill.

367
00:12:21,254 --> 00:12:22,964
That doesn't mean you can never pick

368
00:12:22,964 --> 00:12:23,344
a stock.

369
00:12:23,524 --> 00:12:25,428
But it helps to separate your money.

370
00:12:25,608 --> 00:12:26,809
Keep your serious,

371
00:12:26,809 --> 00:12:29,344
long term money in boring index funds,

372
00:12:29,344 --> 00:12:31,012
and if you want some fun,

373
00:12:31,012 --> 00:12:33,213
use a small amount you can afford

374
00:12:33,213 --> 00:12:33,747
to lose.

375
00:12:34,096 --> 00:12:34,828
Try this.

376
00:12:35,008 --> 00:12:36,775
If you enjoy stock picking,

377
00:12:36,775 --> 00:12:38,739
cap it at five percent of your

378
00:12:38,739 --> 00:12:39,524
investments.

379
00:12:39,704 --> 00:12:42,010
Then compare its results to your index

380
00:12:42,010 --> 00:12:43,466
funds after a few years.

381
00:12:43,646 --> 00:12:45,956
The comparison is often eye opening.

382
00:12:46,306 --> 00:12:47,444
Lesson number nine.

383
00:12:47,624 --> 00:12:49,633
Overconfidence costs money.

384
00:12:49,982 --> 00:12:53,009
Most people believe they're above average drivers,

385
00:12:53,009 --> 00:12:55,915
and many investors believe they're above average

386
00:12:55,915 --> 00:12:56,763
stock pickers.

387
00:12:56,943 --> 00:12:57,988
But by definition,

388
00:12:57,988 --> 00:12:59,961
not everyone can be above average.

389
00:13:00,141 --> 00:13:03,476
Overconfidence leads people to trade more,

390
00:13:03,476 --> 00:13:06,097
take bigger risks and pay more in

391
00:13:06,097 --> 00:13:06,573
costs.

392
00:13:06,923 --> 00:13:09,720
Brad Barber and Terrance Odean studied tens

393
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:12,647
of thousands of household brokerage accounts.

394
00:13:12,827 --> 00:13:14,880
They found that the investors who traded

395
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:17,189
the most earned noticeably lower returns than

396
00:13:17,189 --> 00:13:18,574
those who traded the least.

397
00:13:18,754 --> 00:13:20,623
Their paper's title says it all.

398
00:13:20,803 --> 00:13:22,998
Trading is hazardous to your wealth.

399
00:13:23,347 --> 00:13:26,122
Index investing is a kind of humility.

400
00:13:26,302 --> 00:13:29,122
It accepts that you probably can't predict

401
00:13:29,122 --> 00:13:30,599
which stocks will win,

402
00:13:30,599 --> 00:13:33,419
and turns that humility into an advantage.

403
00:13:33,599 --> 00:13:35,640
You stop competing in a game that's

404
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:36,631
very hard to win,

405
00:13:36,631 --> 00:13:38,905
and simply collect the market's return.

406
00:13:39,254 --> 00:13:39,928
Try this.

407
00:13:40,108 --> 00:13:42,495
Write down every investment decision you make

408
00:13:42,495 --> 00:13:43,927
this year, with the reason.

409
00:13:44,107 --> 00:13:44,889
A year later,

410
00:13:44,889 --> 00:13:46,453
check how they turned out.

411
00:13:46,633 --> 00:13:49,170
Most people find their predictions were far

412
00:13:49,170 --> 00:13:50,882
less reliable than they felt.

413
00:13:51,231 --> 00:13:52,474
Lesson number ten.

414
00:13:52,654 --> 00:13:55,188
Investors often earn less than their own

415
00:13:55,188 --> 00:13:55,568
funds.

416
00:13:55,917 --> 00:13:57,554
Here's something surprising.

417
00:13:57,734 --> 00:14:00,478
Morningstar's Mind the Gap research compares the

418
00:14:00,478 --> 00:14:02,936
returns funds report with the returns their

419
00:14:02,936 --> 00:14:04,480
investors actually receive.

420
00:14:04,660 --> 00:14:07,237
Investors often earn less than the funds

421
00:14:07,237 --> 00:14:09,750
they own, because they buy after prices

422
00:14:09,750 --> 00:14:11,812
rise and sell after prices fall.

423
00:14:11,992 --> 00:14:13,217
In recent studies,

424
00:14:13,217 --> 00:14:16,008
that gap was roughly one percentage point

425
00:14:16,008 --> 00:14:16,485
a year.

426
00:14:16,834 --> 00:14:19,288
One percentage point might not sound like

427
00:14:19,288 --> 00:14:21,502
much, but over decades it's enormous.

428
00:14:21,682 --> 00:14:24,264
One hundred thousand dollars growing at seven

429
00:14:24,264 --> 00:14:26,501
percent a year for thirty years becomes

430
00:14:26,501 --> 00:14:28,624
roughly seven hundred sixty thousand.

431
00:14:28,804 --> 00:14:29,849
At six percent,

432
00:14:29,849 --> 00:14:32,496
roughly five hundred seventy thousand.

433
00:14:32,676 --> 00:14:35,123
Bad timing can cost almost two hundred

434
00:14:35,123 --> 00:14:36,217
thousand dollars.

435
00:14:36,567 --> 00:14:39,122
Index funds don't fix this by themselves.

436
00:14:39,302 --> 00:14:41,162
You can buy and sell an index

437
00:14:41,162 --> 00:14:42,959
fund at the wrong times too.

438
00:14:43,139 --> 00:14:45,518
The advantage comes when you pair index

439
00:14:45,518 --> 00:14:48,386
funds with automatic investing and a commitment

440
00:14:48,386 --> 00:14:48,874
to hold.

441
00:14:49,054 --> 00:14:50,114
The fund is boring,

442
00:14:50,114 --> 00:14:52,012
and your behavior has to be boring

443
00:14:52,012 --> 00:14:52,235
too.

444
00:14:52,585 --> 00:14:53,526
Try this.

445
00:14:53,706 --> 00:14:56,119
Set up automatic monthly investing,

446
00:14:56,119 --> 00:14:58,739
and decide in advance that you'll only

447
00:14:58,739 --> 00:15:01,291
review your portfolio once or twice a

448
00:15:01,291 --> 00:15:01,635
year.

449
00:15:01,815 --> 00:15:04,242
Fewer looks usually mean fewer mistakes.

450
00:15:04,591 --> 00:15:05,578
Quick pause.

451
00:15:05,758 --> 00:15:08,219
If boring investing is starting to make

452
00:15:08,219 --> 00:15:10,994
sense, please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

453
00:15:11,174 --> 00:15:13,039
It's free, and it helps us keep

454
00:15:13,039 --> 00:15:13,821
making these.

455
00:15:14,001 --> 00:15:15,580
Now, lesson eleven.

456
00:15:15,930 --> 00:15:17,184
Lesson number eleven.

457
00:15:17,364 --> 00:15:19,233
Ignore most financial news.

458
00:15:19,583 --> 00:15:21,939
Financial news is designed to keep you

459
00:15:21,939 --> 00:15:22,498
watching.

460
00:15:22,678 --> 00:15:25,038
Markets soar, markets plunge,

461
00:15:25,038 --> 00:15:26,992
experts predict a crash,

462
00:15:26,992 --> 00:15:29,353
other experts predict a boom.

463
00:15:29,533 --> 00:15:31,428
Every day there's a new reason to

464
00:15:31,428 --> 00:15:31,658
act.

465
00:15:31,838 --> 00:15:34,040
But for a long term index investor,

466
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:35,298
most of it is noise.

467
00:15:35,647 --> 00:15:38,178
Predictions are especially unreliable.

468
00:15:38,358 --> 00:15:41,249
Market forecasts from experts have a long

469
00:15:41,249 --> 00:15:42,870
history of being wrong,

470
00:15:42,870 --> 00:15:44,210
in both directions.

471
00:15:44,390 --> 00:15:46,937
If someone could reliably predict the market,

472
00:15:46,937 --> 00:15:49,201
they probably wouldn't be telling you on

473
00:15:49,201 --> 00:15:49,823
television.

474
00:15:50,173 --> 00:15:53,014
The index investor's advantage is not needing

475
00:15:53,014 --> 00:15:54,655
to know what happens next.

476
00:15:54,835 --> 00:15:56,385
You own the whole market,

477
00:15:56,385 --> 00:15:57,626
so whatever happens,

478
00:15:57,626 --> 00:15:59,363
you get the market's result.

479
00:15:59,543 --> 00:16:01,470
That frees you from needing to react

480
00:16:01,470 --> 00:16:02,434
to every headline.

481
00:16:02,783 --> 00:16:03,701
Try this.

482
00:16:03,881 --> 00:16:06,420
Unfollow or mute the market news sources

483
00:16:06,420 --> 00:16:07,816
that make you anxious.

484
00:16:07,996 --> 00:16:10,277
Replace them with one or two good

485
00:16:10,277 --> 00:16:12,489
books about long term investing.

486
00:16:12,839 --> 00:16:14,267
Lesson number twelve.

487
00:16:14,447 --> 00:16:15,817
Fear of missing out.

488
00:16:16,167 --> 00:16:17,944
Every few years there's a new hot

489
00:16:17,944 --> 00:16:18,536
investment.

490
00:16:18,716 --> 00:16:20,007
A sector, a coin,

491
00:16:20,007 --> 00:16:23,046
a single stock everyone's talking about.

492
00:16:23,226 --> 00:16:26,266
Index investors watch other people brag about

493
00:16:26,266 --> 00:16:28,428
huge gains and feel left behind.

494
00:16:28,608 --> 00:16:31,109
That feeling is called fear of missing

495
00:16:31,109 --> 00:16:33,479
out, and it pushes people to abandon

496
00:16:33,479 --> 00:16:34,204
good plans.

497
00:16:34,553 --> 00:16:37,303
The hot investment sometimes does keep rising

498
00:16:37,303 --> 00:16:38,036
for a while.

499
00:16:38,216 --> 00:16:40,190
But many manias end painfully,

500
00:16:40,190 --> 00:16:42,427
and the people who join late often

501
00:16:42,427 --> 00:16:43,545
buy near the top.

502
00:16:43,725 --> 00:16:46,890
Meanwhile, the index investor already owns many

503
00:16:46,890 --> 00:16:49,516
of the companies that become truly big,

504
00:16:49,516 --> 00:16:51,133
without having to guess.

505
00:16:51,482 --> 00:16:53,851
Remember, you only hear about the winners.

506
00:16:54,031 --> 00:16:55,845
The people who lost money on the

507
00:16:55,845 --> 00:16:57,885
last hot thing rarely post about it.

508
00:16:58,065 --> 00:17:00,371
Your picture of how everyone else is

509
00:17:00,371 --> 00:17:01,780
doing is badly biased.

510
00:17:02,130 --> 00:17:02,931
Try this.

511
00:17:03,111 --> 00:17:04,990
When you feel the urge to chase

512
00:17:04,990 --> 00:17:07,779
something hot, wait thirty days before acting.

513
00:17:07,959 --> 00:17:08,893
Most urges fade,

514
00:17:08,893 --> 00:17:11,172
and the ones that don't deserve careful

515
00:17:11,172 --> 00:17:11,639
thought.

516
00:17:11,989 --> 00:17:13,487
Lesson number thirteen.

517
00:17:13,667 --> 00:17:16,697
Average returns create above average results.

518
00:17:17,047 --> 00:17:19,572
Many people hate the idea of settling

519
00:17:19,572 --> 00:17:20,937
for average returns.

520
00:17:21,117 --> 00:17:22,580
But here's the paradox.

521
00:17:22,760 --> 00:17:25,892
Because most active investors trail the market

522
00:17:25,892 --> 00:17:29,092
after costs, simply earning the market's return

523
00:17:29,092 --> 00:17:31,135
puts you ahead of most of them

524
00:17:31,135 --> 00:17:32,361
over long periods.

525
00:17:32,711 --> 00:17:35,014
Think of it like a marathon where

526
00:17:35,014 --> 00:17:37,944
most runners carry heavy backpacks full of

527
00:17:37,944 --> 00:17:40,386
fees, trading costs and bad timing.

528
00:17:40,566 --> 00:17:42,348
You run at an average pace,

529
00:17:42,348 --> 00:17:43,735
but with no backpack.

530
00:17:43,915 --> 00:17:45,754
You end up finishing ahead of most

531
00:17:45,754 --> 00:17:46,458
of the field.

532
00:17:46,807 --> 00:17:48,153
And average returns,

533
00:17:48,153 --> 00:17:49,701
compounded for decades,

534
00:17:49,701 --> 00:17:51,115
can be life changing.

535
00:17:51,295 --> 00:17:53,607
Three hundred dollars a month for thirty

536
00:17:53,607 --> 00:17:55,861
years at a seven percent average yearly

537
00:17:55,861 --> 00:17:58,347
return grows to roughly three hundred sixty

538
00:17:58,347 --> 00:17:59,561
six thousand dollars.

539
00:17:59,741 --> 00:18:01,297
No genius required.

540
00:18:01,646 --> 00:18:02,494
Try this.

541
00:18:02,674 --> 00:18:05,728
Stop measuring yourself against the best investor

542
00:18:05,728 --> 00:18:06,726
you've heard of.

543
00:18:06,906 --> 00:18:09,009
Measure yourself against what you'd have if

544
00:18:09,009 --> 00:18:10,133
you did nothing at all.

545
00:18:10,313 --> 00:18:11,962
That's the real comparison.

546
00:18:12,312 --> 00:18:13,856
Lesson number fourteen.

547
00:18:14,036 --> 00:18:16,382
Fewer decisions, better results.

548
00:18:16,731 --> 00:18:19,180
Every investing decision is a chance to

549
00:18:19,180 --> 00:18:20,121
make a mistake.

550
00:18:20,301 --> 00:18:22,060
Which stock, when to buy,

551
00:18:22,060 --> 00:18:22,974
when to sell,

552
00:18:22,974 --> 00:18:25,085
which manager, which strategy.

553
00:18:25,265 --> 00:18:28,144
Index investing removes most of those decisions.

554
00:18:28,324 --> 00:18:30,034
You choose a few funds and a

555
00:18:30,034 --> 00:18:31,621
mix, and then keep buying.

556
00:18:31,971 --> 00:18:34,955
Fewer decisions also means less stress.

557
00:18:35,135 --> 00:18:37,663
You don't have to research companies every

558
00:18:37,663 --> 00:18:40,371
